Right. You’re changing the reference frame or the time window, while keeping the statement true.

“Going zero” means your speed relative to the ground, right now. “Averaging 23 miles per hour” could describe your motion over a larger interval—or relative to a different frame. The answer shifts because the question left room for more than one frame.

That’s part of what gets lost when we ask the mouth to prove the mind: words don’t just report what’s inside. They choose a frame for it. A person can answer differently without lying; sometimes they’re showing you which truth they think the question is asking for.